Current State
PDFs are stored per user (in user-specific directories), but the user ID is currently passed from the client, which is insecure.
No strong enforcement that only the authenticated user can access, list, or delete their own PDFs.
Crawls and PDF generation are not strictly linked to the authenticated user session.
What Needs to Be Done
Derive user ID from authentication, not from the client.
Use authentication middleware (e.g., JWT from NextAuth) to extract the user ID on the backend.
Remove all uses of userId from client-supplied data in API requests.
Enforce user association in all relevant endpoints:
When generating, listing, downloading, or deleting PDFs, always use the user ID from the session/token.
Ensure crawls and progress tracking are also tied to the authenticated user.
(Optional) Store user ID in metadata:
When saving PDF metadata, include the user ID for traceability.
Example Implementation Steps
Add authentication middleware to your Express backend that verifies the JWT/session and sets req.userId.
Update all endpoints to use req.userId for file paths and queries.
Remove userId from client API calls (frontend should not send it).
(Optional) Update metadata to include the user ID.
Example: Enforcing User Association
Apply
// In your Express route handlers:
const userId = req.userId; // Set by authentication middleware

// Use this userId for all file operations:
const userPdfPath = path.join(pdfStoragePath, userId, ...);
Summary
Goal: Only the authenticated user can access, create, list, or delete their own PDFs and crawls.
How: Always use the user ID from the session/token, never from the client.
